The bug: `compileCondition` uses `condition[key].map((child, index) => ...)` — `.map` skips holes in sparse arrays, so holes in `all`/`any` arrays pass validation and produce a compiled children array with holes. Then at runtime `runCondition` accesses `compiled.children[i]` which would be `undefined` for the hole... Actually `.map` on sparse array preserves holes, so `compiled.children[i]` is `undefined` for hole positions, and `runCondition(undefined, ...)` would throw `compiled.kind` TypeError at runtime. But if short-circuit happens before reaching the hole, it's skipped.

Fix: in `compileCondition`, check for holes in `condition[key]` — for each index 0..length-1, if `!Object.hasOwn(condition[key], index)`, throw error like `condition node ${nodeId} at ${position}.${key}[${index}]: missing sub-condition` or similar. The task says: error should carry condition node id and accurate child position, e.g. `$.any[1]`; nested positions preserved with full hierarchy from root.

Note: explicit `undefined`/`null` values are handled by existing rules — `isUsableObject(undefined)` fails → "condition must be an object" error at that position. Holes are different: `Object.hasOwn` returns false for a hole but true for explicit undefined.

Also "只有长度、没有任何元素的数组" — array with length but all holes — should be rejected too. The hole check covers that.

Also "不能改动调用者提供的条件数组和工作流定义" — we don't mutate; just validate.

Implementation: in the `all`/`any` branch, before mapping, iterate indices:

```js
const list = condition[key];
if (!Array.isArray(list)) ...
if (list.length === 0) ...
for (let index = 0; index < list.length; index += 1) {
  if (!Object.hasOwn(list, index)) {
    throw new Error(`condition node ${nodeId} at ${position}.${key}[${index}]: missing sub-condition`);
  }
}
```

